{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And the children of Israel.</b> The reason this is mentioned again is that Pharaoh was drowning while men of Israel were still crossing in the sea, and this was a wonder within a wonder: for in the place where Israel was crossing the sea, an east wind was there drying it, while in the place of Pharaoh and his army, HaShem brought a different wind to melt the waters that had congealed and become walls. And so there were two winds in the sea and the two places were close together. And the trustworthy witness to this interpretation is what is written in the song: <i>and with the breath of Your nostrils the waters piled up</i> (below 15:8), and this wind was with Israel, and it is written <i>You blew with Your wind, the sea covered them</i> (ibid. v. 10), and this is the one that drowned the Egyptians; therefore at the end of the song it is written <i>and HaShem brought back upon them the waters of the sea</i>, <i>but the children of Israel walked on dry land in the midst of the sea</i> (ibid. v. 19). And do not wonder how they could cross the entire night — and could six hundred thousand foot soldiers have been but a quarter of the camp? Perhaps they crossed by a different route, or it was torn into twelve parts as the Sages said, and Israel did not cross the sea along its width but entered along its length and came out to the wilderness of Shur, where they had entered, as I shall explain:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:34:37.726913033Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}